{
  "review": "Fulci playing a version of himself, writing down some ideas for how people could die. Followed by a fake-looking cat eating what is presumably a brain. The copy I watched was dubbed in English, which I always hate, but I was particularly disappointed not to get to hear Fulci in his own voice.\n\nFulci is in a sort of feverish state working on his latest horror movie. His stomach turns when he sees things that resemble effects from his movie, and he starts to hallucinate that he is witnessing acts of horror. He visits a psychiatrist, who hypnotizes him and unfortunately he does not have his improved mental health in mind. I was reminded of the psychiatrist played by David Cronenberg in Clive Barker's Nightbreed (1990). The shrink in this one is played by David L. Thompson, who is pretty bad. Probably a real life friend of Fulci's, he has a big toothy grin when he kills people, though this may be Fulci's black humor at work which I thought was pretty poor too.\n\nThe movie is composed of a lot of clips from Fulci's movies, either as if Fulci is on the set directing them, watching recordings on TVs, or witnessing the acts. I've never been too much of a fan of clip shows in TV series, and I also think things like Charles Band's Full Moon Entertainment cutting their old films down and putting three such cuts together as new anthologies are pretty lame. I guess they need to make money?\n\nThe shrink in Cat in the Brain makes reference to the theory that violence in movies begets violence in real life. One of Fulci's co-workers talks about having a documentary crew follow Fulci to see what his life is like. Lots of self-referential stuff like this.\n\nIn the end, some of the characters sail away on a boat named \"Perversion.\"",
  "translate": "Fulci si presenta lui stesso, scrivendo alcune idee su come le persone potrebbero morire. Segue un gatto fittizio che mangia presumibilmente il cervello. La versione che ho visto era doppiata in inglese, che odio sempre, ma ero particolarmente deluso di non riuscire ad ascoltare Fulci nella sua propria voce.\n\nFulci è in uno stato febbrile lavorando al suo ultimo film horror. La sua pancia si volta quando vede cose che assomigliano agli effetti dal suo film, e comincia a sognare di assistere ad atti di orrore. Visita un medico, che lo ipnotizza e non riesce a ottenere miglioramenti nella sua salute mentale.\n\nIl film è composto da molti clip dalle sue pellicole, o come se Fulci fosse in scena, dirigendo, guardando registrazioni su TV o assistendo agli atti. Non sono un grande fan di clip shows in serie TV e penso che anche le azioni di Charles Band's Full Moon Entertainment per creare nuove anthologie tagliando vecchi film siano piuttosto stancanti.\n\nIl medico in Cat in the Brain fa riferimento alla teoria che la violenza nei film attiva la violenza nella vita reale. Uno dei colleghi di Fulci parla di un team documentario che segue Fulci per vedere cosa sia la sua vita. Molta roba auto-referenziale.\n\nAlla fine, alcuni personaggi partono su una barca chiamata \"Perversion.\"",
